h1. Proposed configuration, by architecture

{info}
Generated by scripts/build-architectures.py on 2026-09-02. Do not hand-edit: every core list, kernel argument and diagram below is rendered from the planner, so it is what scripts/install.sh would actually write for that shape. Regenerate after any change to profiles/policy.json or a profile.
{info}

One layered diagram per supported metal shape, each carrying that shape's real core allocation and the actual values every layer installs: the kernel arguments, the cgroup cpusets, the runtime pinning targets and the core contract handed to the application.

{toc:maxLevel=2|minLevel=2}

h2. How to read the diagrams

Up to three diagrams per shape - the NIC distance axis is only drawn where there is more than one NUMA node to rank. All of them are 78 columns wide and rendered from the same plan the installer uses.

||Diagram||What it shows||What to check in it||
|the layer stack|the four layers, with this shape's real values in each|that pulsar.slice and isolcpus carry the same list|
|the core map|one segmented bar per NUMA node, ordered nearest-NIC-first; the dividers are where each role's cut actually falls|that every node has its own housekeeping and IRQ cores|
|the NIC distance axis|the exclusive pool split by distance from the primary NIC, with each column sized by how many cores it holds|how narrow the NIC-local column is - that is the real latency budget|

Read the layer stack downward. Layer 4 is what the application sees; layer 0 is the machine underneath. The constraint drawn between layers 2 and 1 is the only hard one in the design - a pulsar.slice that disagreed with isolcpus would keep serving traffic while its tail latency doubled, and nothing at run time would say so.

The four layers, and what each one is:

||Layer||Mechanism||When it takes effect||
|4 - application core contract|the two core lists the app pins its own threads to|start-up; no reboot|
|3 - runtime pinning|IRQ affinity, ENA queue count, XPS, workqueues, governor|every boot|
|2 - cgroup v2 slices|AllowedCPUs on the managed slices|daemon-reload; immediate|
|1 - boot isolation|isolcpus, nohz_full, rcu_nocbs, irqaffinity|requires a reboot|
|0 - machine|the cores the shape actually has|-|

h2. All seven at a glance

||Architecture||Metal SKU||CPU||Cores||NUMA||hk||irq||shared||exclusive||NIC-local exclusive||
|{{c7i-24xl}}|{{c7i.metal-24xl}}|Intel Sapphire Rapids|48|1|2|2|7|37|37|
|{{c7i-48xl}}|{{c7i.metal-48xl}}|Intel Sapphire Rapids|96|2|4|4|14|74|37|
|{{c8i-48xl}}|{{c8i.metal-48xl}}|Intel Xeon 6 (Granite Rapids)|96|3|6|6|15|69|23|
|{{c8i-96xl}}|{{c8i.metal-96xl}}|Intel Xeon 6975P-C|192|6|12|12|30|138|23|
|{{c7a-48xl}}|{{c7a.metal-48xl}}|AMD EPYC 9R14 (Genoa)|192|2|8|10|30|144|72|
|{{c8a-24xl}}|{{c8a.metal-24xl}}|AMD EPYC Turin|96|1|4|5|15|72|72|
|{{c8a-48xl}}|{{c8a.metal-48xl}}|AMD EPYC Turin|192|2|8|10|30|144|72|

Policy: shared_ratio=0.15, min_exclusive_ratio=0.55, l3_align=true, cstate_max=1. Housekeeping and IRQ cores are reserved per NUMA node, which is why node count and not core count drives the platform's overhead. The last column is the part of the exclusive pool that is local to the primary NIC - on c8i-96xl that is 23 of 138 cores, and it is the number that actually bounds the latency-critical shard.

h2. NIC locality: which NUMA node is closest to the primary NIC

Exclusive cores are not interchangeable. The primary ENA adapter hangs off one PCIe root complex, which belongs to one NUMA node, and every packet descriptor and DMA buffer touched from another node pays a die crossing or a socket hop. Core isolation does not buy that back. So the planner ranks every node by its distance from the NIC and hands the application the exclusive cores in that order: tier 0 first.

The ranking is computed, not configured. On a live host it comes from the ACPI SLIT plus the adapter's own numa_node. From a profile there is no SLIT to read, so it falls back to the structural model - the NIC's own node, then the rest of that socket, then the other socket.

||Architecture||NUMA nodes||NIC node||Status||NIC-local exclusive||Node order, nearest first||
|{{c7i-24xl}}|1|node0|certain|37 of 37|node0|
|{{c7i-48xl}}|2|node0|inferred|37 of 74|node0 > node1|
|{{c8i-48xl}}|3|node0|inferred|23 of 69|node0 > node1 > node2|
|{{c8i-96xl}}|6|node0|inferred|23 of 138|node0 > node1 > node2 > node3 > node4 > node5|
|{{c7a-48xl}}|2|node0|inferred|72 of 144|node0 > node1|
|{{c8a-24xl}}|1|node0|certain|72 of 72|node0|
|{{c8a-48xl}}|2|node0|inferred|72 of 144|node0 > node1|

Why each shape has the status it has:

* {{c7i-24xl}}: single NUMA node - the NIC is local to it whatever it hangs off
* {{c7i-48xl}}: AWS publishes no PCIe root-complex map, and the socket the primary ENA hangs off is a platform wiring choice. Socket 0 is the convention.
* {{c8i-48xl}}: Under SNC3 the PCIe root ports live on the I/O dies that flank the three compute dies, so firmware assigns the NIC to an EDGE domain - node 0 or node 2, not the middle die. Which of the two is a wiring choice AWS does not publish.
* {{c8i-96xl}}: Under SNC3 the PCIe root ports live on the I/O dies that flank the three compute dies, so firmware assigns the NIC to an EDGE domain of one socket - node 0 or node 2 if that socket is socket 0.
* {{c7a-48xl}}: AWS publishes no PCIe root-complex map, and the socket the primary ENA hangs off is a platform wiring choice. Socket 0 is the convention.
* {{c8a-24xl}}: single NUMA node - the NIC is local to it whatever it hangs off
* {{c8a-48xl}}: AWS publishes no PCIe root-complex map, and the socket the primary ENA hangs off is a platform wiring choice. Socket 0 is the convention.

{warning:title=The NIC node is inferred on every multi-node shape}
AWS publishes no PCIe root-complex map, so which node the primary NIC sits on is an inference on every multi-node shape until a real host has been read. Run 'lltune nic --live' on first contact with a shape and correct the profile. 'lltune validate' fails if the installed plan's NIC node disagrees with the host, because a wrong NIC node turns the whole ranking upside down.
{warning}

How to settle it on a real host of the shape:

{code:language=bash}
lltune nic --live        # adapter, its NUMA node, the SLIT, and the ranking
lltune validate          # fails if the installed plan assumed the wrong node
{code}

If the adapter reports numa_node = -1 the firmware exposed no _PXM for it. That is not a shape without NIC locality; it is a shape whose locality you cannot read, and the plan falls back to node 0. Treat the ranking as unproven and measure it instead - pin a load generator to one node at a time and compare.

